Add a Menu and Menu Commands
You can add a menu and menu commands to provide access to functions of your dialog.
To add a menu to your dialog:
• Right-click the tab Elements and select New Menu/New Menu Command.
• Enter the Name of the menu. A Method object can call this dialog item using the Name you entered.
• Enter the Caption, which the menu displays. A caption is especially handy if you develop libraries in
several languages and want the user to be able to switch languages. In addition you can enter special
characters and blanks. In our example we entered &Show. The ampersand & designates the access key.
Note
For the top menu itself, you do not have to enter a Callback Argument.
Add a Menu and Menu Commands
Plant Simulation Help 10-1415
Unpublished work. © 2026 Siemens

Note
The display window of the object Dialog that contains menus does not use the standard Siemens PLM
theme, but your Windows theme.
The display window of the object Dialog that does not contain menus uses the standard Siemens PLM
theme.
Add a Menu and Menu Commands
10-1416 Plant Simulation Help
Unpublished work. © 2026 Siemens

To add a menu command to the menu you added above:
• Right-click the name of the menu on the tab Elements and select New Menu/Menu Command.
• Enter the Name of the menu command.
• Enter the Caption, which the menu command displays. In our examples we entered &Chart and
&Report. The ampersand & designates the access key.
If you want to use the ampersand character & instead of and in a dialog item, for example Drag & Drop,
type in the & twice, Drag && Drop.
Add a Menu and Menu Commands
Plant Simulation Help 10-1417
Unpublished work. © 2026 Siemens

• To make that the dialog open the Chart or the Report, when the user selects Show > Chart or Show
> Report, you have to program it to do so. For this you have to enter the Callback Argument,
which will be passed to the callback method. In our examples we entered CallbackChart and
CallbackReport.
• Then, enter the code for actually opening the Chart or the Report into the callback method. As the
callback method is a user-defined attribute of data type method, click the tab User-defined.
Double-click callback and click Open in the dialog User-defined Attributes. To be able to enter
commands into the Method, click Inherit Source Code on the Edit ribbon tab of the Method so that it is
not selected 
 .
The source code looks like this:
param action: string
switch action
case "Open"
                // TODO: add code for the "Open" action here
                // for example ?.setCaption("TextBox", "Test")
                // for example ?.setCheckBox("CheckBox", true)
case "Apply"
                // TODO: add code for the "Apply" action here
                // for example print ?.getValue("TextBox")
Add a Menu and Menu Commands
10-1418 Plant Simulation Help
Unpublished work. © 2026 Siemens

                // for example print ?.GetCheckBox("CheckBox")
case "Close"
                // TODO: add code for the "Close" action here
end
Double-click callback and click Open in the dialog User-defined Attributes.
To open the object MyChart, we entered:
case "CallbackChart" 
   MyChart.IsShown := true 
To open the object MyReport, we entered:
case "CallbackReport" 
   MyReport.show 
• To change the order of the menu commands on a menu, select a menu command on the tab
Elements, hold down Shift and press the Up arrow to move this menu command up or press the Down
arrow to move this menu command down.
• To add a submenu to the selected menu command, right click that menu command and select New
Menu/New Menu Command again. Repeat the steps described above. A submenu looks like this.
